<ArticleLayout slug="production-roster-review-questions" ctaLabel="Book a roster review call" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Advertising production is being pulled out of individual agencies and into centralized units. Your contracts were written against the old shape. Here is the instrument for finding out what actually changed.</p>

  <h2>What is a production roster review, and what should it ask?</h2>

  <p>A production roster review is a structured reassessment of every partner who makes your advertising assets, run when your supply base changes shape rather than when a contract happens to expire. Production here means advertising production: images, films, platform variants. It asks four things. Whether your contract still binds who you thought it bound, how the commercials work now, where the bidding conflicts sit, and what leaves with you. hubStudio published the fourteen questions below so buyers can score partners consistently.</p>

  <p>Most of what is written about rosters answers a different question. It tells procurement how many agencies to keep, or it lists things to ask a video company you are about to hire for the first time. Both are useful. Neither helps the marketing director who already has six production partners, has just been told that three of them now report into the same centralized unit, and has to decide by the end of the quarter whether that is a problem.</p>

  <p>If that is you, start at the four groups and come back for the evidence. The questions work without it. They are simply harder to insist on.</p>

  <h2>What changed in production supply, and when</h2>

  <p>Something structural moved between 2025 and early 2026, and it moved fast enough that most rosters have not been re-examined since.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Between 2025 and early 2026 the largest agency groups each folded production teams that used to sit inside separate agencies into one centralized production unit. Source: trade coverage of the category, February 2026, a count of four publicly announced reorganizations, no group named here.</blockquote>

  <p>Read that as a supply-side fact and it is unremarkable. Groups reorganize constantly. Read it as a buyer and it is more interesting, because the distributed model it replaced was the one your contracts were written against. Under that model each agency ran its own production capability, and when you appointed the agency you appointed the studio behind it. The two came together. Now they can come apart.</p>

  <p>The buy side was already moving in the same direction, and it was measured.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Centralization was named as a returning production model, alongside the rise of outsourced teams sitting on the client's own site. Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, a qualitative trend finding across the same 50 brands, with no figure attached.</blockquote>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Two thirds of the brands surveyed had changed their agency model within the previous four years. Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, same survey of 50 global brands, benchmarked against the same study run in 2021.</blockquote>

  <p>Two thirds, in four years. Whatever your roster looked like when you signed it, the odds are it does not look like that now.</p>

  <p>And the pot being fought over got smaller while the work got harder.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Production accounted for an average of 19 percent of total marketing budgets, against 24 percent before the pandemic. Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, a survey of 50 global brands across more than ten sectors answered mainly by senior marketing procurement leads.</blockquote>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Nearly two thirds of the brands surveyed expect to increase social and short-form video production. Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, same survey of 50 global brands.</blockquote>

  <p>Five points of budget share gone, volume expectations up. That is the pressure that makes consolidation attractive to a supplier, and it is why the questions below lean hard on commercials.</p>

  <p>One more, because it changes who is even on the roster:</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">One third of the brands surveyed now build production relationships directly with the digital platforms. Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, same survey of 50 global brands.</blockquote>

  <h2>Why a roster review is the moment production budgets actually move</h2>

  <p>Because it is the only moment they can. Production spend is sticky in a way media spend is not. It is attached to relationships, to file archives, to people who know the product, and to a hundred small conventions nobody wrote down. Left alone, it renews.</p>

  <p>A structural change to your supply base is the event that legitimately reopens it. Not a bad quarter, not a new CMO. A change in who owns the studio.</p>

  <p>The other reason to do it now is that the last time anyone published a buy-side measurement of how production was actually being bought, the answer was uncomfortable.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Fewer than half of the advertisers surveyed required their agency to disclose whether a production job was being bid to an in-house or affiliated production company. Source: US advertiser trade body production transparency study, August 2017, built on a task force of 30 member executives and 12 subject-matter organizations plus a supporting member survey whose respondent count was not published.</blockquote>

  <blockquote class="evidence">More than 60 percent of the advertisers surveyed either did not require, or did not know whether their agency contract required, production rebates and other incentives to be passed back to them. Source: US advertiser trade body production transparency study, August 2017, same survey, respondent count not published.</blockquote>

  <p>Those figures are from 2017. They describe 2017 practice, not 2026 practice, and both are percentages with no absolute figure published behind them. Treat them as the last published buy-side measurement of production bidding and rebate terms rather than as a description of your roster today.</p>

  <p>The reason they are still worth putting in front of a partner is that the question they measured has not gone away.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The UK advertiser trade body brought its marketing procurement members together to examine competitive bidding in production and the growth of agency-owned production companies. Source: UK advertiser trade body marketing procurement session, January 2024, a members' panel, no figures published.</blockquote>

  <p>Seven years after the study, the trade bodies were still convening members on the same problem. That is not a solved issue. That is an open one that stopped being reported.</p>

  <h2>What are the four things a roster review has to establish?</h2>

  <p>Fourteen questions, four groups. The grouping matters more than the count, because each group fails differently and each one is owned by a different person on your side.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Group</th>
          <th scope="col">What it establishes</th>
          <th scope="col">Who owns it on your side</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Contract continuity</th>
          <td>Whether the paper still binds who you think it binds</td>
          <td>Legal, with procurement</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Commercials</th>
          <td>How the price is actually built, and who else is paid</td>
          <td>Procurement</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Bidding conflict</th>
          <td>Whether the party advising the spend also receives it</td>
          <td>Marketing, with procurement</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Platform and data exit</th>
          <td>What you can take with you, and in what state</td>
          <td>Marketing operations</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Ask them of every partner on the roster. That includes the incumbent you like, the one you inherited, and the one writing this. A question set that exempts the author is a sales document, not an instrument.</p>

  <h2>Group one: does the contract still bind who you think it binds?</h2>

  <p>This group exists because a reorganization can change the entity delivering your work without changing the entity on your contract. Sometimes that is fine. You need to know which it is.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Question</th>
          <th scope="col">What a good answer sounds like</th>
          <th scope="col">What a weak answer sounds like</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Which legal entity delivers our work today, and is it the entity we contracted with?</th>
          <td>Names both, confirms they match, or explains precisely where they diverged and when</td>
          <td>"It is all the same group"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Has our work moved between entities or units since we signed, and were we told?</th>
          <td>A dated list of what moved and when, volunteered before you asked</td>
          <td>"Nothing material has changed"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Does our agreement have a change-of-control or assignment clause, and what does it let you do without our consent?</th>
          <td>Quotes the clause and says what it permits</td>
          <td>Offers to check and come back</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Who is our named production lead, and where does that person sit in the new structure?</th>
          <td>A name, a reporting line, and what happens if they leave</td>
          <td>A team inbox</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The last one deserves a note. It is tempting to assume that a centralized unit changes who your producer answers to, and that this changes how your work gets prioritized. That is a reasonable worry. It is not a documented fact, and no buy-side or supply-side source establishes it as a pattern. So ask it. Do not assume the answer.</p>

  <h2>Group two: how is the price actually built?</h2>

  <p>The commercial group is where the 2017 findings still bite. Not because the numbers are current, but because the mechanisms they exposed are still available to anyone who wants to use them.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Question</th>
          <th scope="col">What a good answer sounds like</th>
          <th scope="col">What a weak answer sounds like</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Show us the cost build for our last three jobs, line by line.</th>
          <td>Sends them, unprompted, with labor separated from pass-through</td>
          <td>"We work to an agreed rate card"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Which lines are marked up, and by how much?</th>
          <td>A number, applied consistently, disclosed in the contract</td>
          <td>"Our fees are all-in"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Do you receive rebates, incentives, or volume credits from any supplier, platform, or production service used on our work? If so, what happens to them?</th>
          <td>Discloses them and states the pass-back mechanism</td>
          <td>"We do not take rebates" with nothing in the contract to that effect</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">How does your pricing respond when our volume drops by half?</th>
          <td>Sets out the fixed and variable split, including what stays fixed</td>
          <td>"We would work with you on that"</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>That last question is the one most likely to produce a useful silence. A partner with real fixed costs behind your account will say so. A partner whose price is mostly variable can tell you that and prove it from the build.</p>

  <p>The rebate question is worth asking even when you are confident of the answer. Its value is that it converts an assumption into a contractual position. The 2017 finding was not that most advertisers were being cheated. It was that most advertisers did not know, and had not written down, what should happen.</p>

  <h2>Group three: is the party recommending the spend also receiving it?</h2>

  <p>This is the oldest question in production procurement and the one that centralization makes sharper, because a group with one production unit has an obvious internal home for any job it recommends.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Question</th>
          <th scope="col">What you are listening for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">When you run a competitive bid, do any of the bidders belong to your group, and do you tell us before the bid or after?</th>
          <td>Before, always, in writing, as standard practice. "We disclose on request" means nobody has ever requested it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">How many genuinely independent bids did our last three projects receive, and who were the losing bidders?</th>
          <td>Numbers and roles, even anonymized. A general assurance that everything is competitively bid is not an answer to a question about three specific projects</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Who writes the specification the bid is run against?</th>
          <td>A named role, sitting apart from any party that can win the work</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">If we brought in a production partner from outside your group, would you brief them, and on what terms?</th>
          <td>Willingness, on the same terms, with a coordination fee scoped in advance. Watch for a fee set high enough to make the option theoretical</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The specification question is the load-bearing one, and it is the one buyers skip. Competitive bidding is easy to defeat without breaking a single rule. You write a specification that only one bidder can meet comfortably, then you run a scrupulously fair process against it. Every bid is genuine. The outcome was decided before any of them arrived. So ask who holds the pen.</p>

  <h2>Group four: what leaves with you, and in what state?</h2>

  <p>Every production partner now runs your assets through some system. A digital asset manager, an automation platform, a generation pipeline, often all three stacked. The exit questions used to be about tapes and archives. Now they are about formats and metadata, and about what rights travel with the file.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Question</th>
          <th scope="col">What a good answer sounds like</th>
          <th scope="col">What a weak answer sounds like</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">If we leave in ninety days, what exactly do we receive?</th>
          <td>A named list: layered masters, source files, project files, fonts, metadata, provenance records, with formats</td>
          <td>"All your assets"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Do we get editable source files or only flattened outputs?</th>
          <td>Editable, in a stated format, as a contractual deliverable</td>
          <td>Flattened exports, or "the working files stay in our system"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Where is our material stored, under which jurisdiction, and for how long after we leave?</th>
          <td>Named locations, a stated retention period, a deletion commitment</td>
          <td>Vagueness about the cloud</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">For any AI-assisted work, what provenance record comes with each asset, and does it survive export?</th>
          <td>A per-asset record with model, references, rights status, human edits, and approver, and a statement of what survives download</td>
          <td>"Everything is compliant"</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The final question separates partners quickly, and it will keep separating them as disclosure regimes tighten in more markets. A partner who has never been asked will tell you the work is compliant. A partner who has built for it will show you the record schema.</p>

