  <p>Sixteen rows for fourteen questions. The two exit questions about files and storage each split into two rows, because a partner can pass one half and fail the other, and averaging that hides the failure. Keep them split.</p>

  <p>Run it as a written exercise, not a conversation. Written answers can be compared side by side and attached to a contract. Conversations cannot, and the half-answer that sounded reasonable across a table tends not to survive being typed out.</p>

  <p>The question set was last revised in September 2026. The evidence under it carries its own dates, which is the part worth checking before you rely on any of it, here or anywhere else.</p>

  <h2>What do you do with a roster that scores badly?</h2>

  <p>Usually not what people expect, which is to fire someone.</p>

  <p>A low score is most often a documentation problem rather than a conduct problem. Nobody ever asked, so nothing got written down, so nothing can be evidenced now. The fix is a contract amendment. Amendments are cheap. A pitch is the most expensive thing in marketing procurement, it eats months of your team's attention, and it frequently ends with a partner who cannot answer these questions either.</p>

  <p>Reserve it for the two cases that warrant it: a partner who will not disclose a bidding relationship, and a partner who cannot commit to an exit position. Those are structural. Everything else is paperwork you have not done yet.</p>

  <p>Worth saying plainly, since this is published by a production company. We answer the same sixteen rows. The <a href="/solutions/brands">brands solutions page</a> and the <a href="/work">work index</a> set out how the engagements are structured, and the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> states the commercial model in the same terms group two asks for. If any of it does not survive the questions, that is useful to you either way.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about roster reviews</h2>

  <h3>What is a production roster review?</h3>

  <p>A structured reassessment of every partner who makes your advertising assets. It is different from an agency pitch, which replaces a partner, and different from a vendor selection, which adds one. A roster review examines the partners you already have, usually because something changed in how production supply is organized rather than because a contract expired.</p>

  <h3>How often should we review our production roster?</h3>

  <p>Whenever the structure of your supply changes, and otherwise every two to three years. Structural triggers matter more than the calendar: a partner reorganizing, your work moving between entities, a shift in where your volume sits, or a new disclosure obligation in a market you sell into.</p>

  <h3>What does production consolidation mean for a brand?</h3>

  <p>It means production capability that used to sit inside individual agencies now sits in a shared unit. Trade coverage in February 2026 counted four such reorganizations among the largest groups between 2025 and early 2026. The practical consequence is that the entity making your work may no longer be the entity you contracted with, which is what group one of these questions tests.</p>

  <h3>Should procurement or marketing run the review?</h3>

  <p>Both, on different groups. Legal and procurement own contract continuity and commercials. Marketing owns bidding conflict, because only marketing can judge whether a specification was written to favor one bidder. Marketing operations owns exit, because only they know what a usable file actually is.</p>

  <h3>Do these questions apply to an in-house studio?</h3>

  <p>Most of them. Contract continuity does not apply, and the bidding group changes shape, but the commercial questions apply with more force, because an in-house unit rarely has its cost build examined at all. The exit questions apply unchanged, since an in-house team still runs your assets through systems you may not control.</p>

  <h3>What is a production rebate?</h3>

  <p>A payment, credit, or incentive that flows from a supplier back to the party that placed the work, rather than to the advertiser who paid for it. Volume credits from a rental house, a post facility, or a platform all count. The question for your contract is not whether they exist but where they land, and group two above is where you settle it in writing.</p>
